# The hand-labeled sample behind the second figure in ../accuracy.md.
#
# pallets/click at 874ca2bc1c30d93a4ac6e36a15ed685eafe89097 ships nine example
# programs under `examples/`. Its own test suite never imports any of them, so
# nothing there is ever observed at runtime. These five were read by hand and
# every call written down, so the region has ground truth of a different kind
# from a trace: somebody looking at the source rather than a running process.
#
# Method, stated so it can be disagreed with:
#
#   * One line per call, written down by reading the file. Nothing was taken
#     from the graph, and the file was not re-read after the graph was built.
#   * A line is `<file>:<qualified name> -> <callee>`, the way the graph names
#     both ends. The callee is the expression that is called, with an import
#     resolved to the module it came from: `from urllib import parse as
#     urlparse` is labeled `urllib.parse.urlparse`, because that is what the
#     call reaches.
#   * A decorator is labeled as a call from the function it decorates. It runs
#     at import time rather than when the function is called, so a reader could
#     reasonably put it at module scope instead; the convention is the graph's,
#     and it is called out here because it is the one choice in this file that
#     could move the figure.
#   * A call at module scope is labeled from the module itself, as in
#     `completion.py:completion -> cli.add_command`.
#   * A call is labeled whether or not its name can be resolved. `f.read` and
#     `self.fail` are calls to something, and refusing to name a callee would
#     quietly measure the pipeline's resolver rather than its inference.
#   * The same call at two call sites is one edge, written once.
#
# What is deliberately not here: the eight one-line `setup.py` files, which
# contain no calls, and the four larger examples, which are not small enough to
# label exhaustively and honestly without more time than this is worth. The
# sample is a sample, and its size is stated in the report rather than implied.
#
# What this sample cannot show, stated here so it is not a surprise in the
# report: every call in these files goes to something the repository does not
# define, `click.echo` and `os.listdir` among them. The pipeline writes down
# the name it read in the source and stops, so the figure it produces measures
# whether the calls were found, not whether their targets were resolved. The
# trace figure in the report is the one that exercises resolution, because a
# call that really happened has a real callee to match against.

# examples/colors/colors.py
examples/colors/colors.py:cli -> click.command
examples/colors/colors.py:cli -> click.echo
examples/colors/colors.py:cli -> click.style

